<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geneva</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Genava, Genève</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geneva is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> flows out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>. As Genava it was a frontier town of the Allobroges, mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 58 BCE, and later a Roman and episcopal city. In the 16th century it became the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and today it hosts the European headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and the Red Cross.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geneva.jpg" alt="Geneva" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: fr:Utilisateur:Stéphane_Pecorini · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geneve_2005_001_Ork.ch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Weak in the fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, Savoy suffered its worst disaster in 1536, when Francis I of France occupied Savoy and most of Piedmont, Bern conquered the Vaud and <b>Geneva</b> established its independence under the Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great St Bernard Pass and other Alpine crossings linked it with Italy, the Rhône carried traffic between the Mediterranean and the north, and cities such as Lyon, Vienne, Arles, Avignon, <b>Geneva</b> and, above all, Marseille grew as commercial centres from the eleventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the sixteenth century textiles gained in importance, with linen around St Gallen, silk and cotton in Zürich and Basel, while Protestant refugees brought skills such as watchmaking to <b>Geneva</b> and the Jura.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The so-called Long Diet, sitting in Zurich from 1814 to 1815, negotiated a new Federal Treaty, sworn on 7 August 1815, which bound twenty-two cantons together, including the newly admitted Valais, Neuchâtel and <b>Geneva</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1762 Rousseau’s The Social Contract and Emile were banned in France and <b>Geneva</b>, and Rousseau fled to escape arrest.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>John Calvin (1509–1564), a French lawyer and humanist who had fled persecution in France, came to <b>Geneva</b> in 1536, was expelled in 1538 and returned in 1541, when the city adopted his Ecclesiastical Ordinances.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li></ul></section>
